# Chapter 50 The Broads of the Common Chord and the Board Laid Bare

Date: 2026-08-25

## Summary

The party's departure from the Spire of the Morninglord was interrupted by the arrival of Aurelan Vance, a representative of the Counting House who had been inspired by Valphine's recent sermon to take action. Breathless from rushing over from the bank, Aurelan — Brewbarry's banker and reluctant agent — straightened his clothes before greeting the goliath. Brewbarry invited him to dispense with formal titles and call him “Brew,” prompting Aurelan to offer his own familiar name: “And since we're on such friendly terms, my friends call me Ori.”

Aurelan shared what he had uncovered about a gnome named Bimble. Bimble had been carried on a commission maintenance roll for eleven years, but payments had stopped abruptly seven tendays ago with no severance, no final reconciliation, and no forwarding instructions. “That is not how a contract ends,” Aurelan insisted. “That is how a contract is abandoned!” Bimble had drawn nothing since: he had not banked, borrowed, or left the city. Aurelan concluded with the certainty of a man who only knew one kind of question: “Find who feeds a man, and you have found the man.”

The party decided to seek out Lim, the proprietor of The Board Laid Bare and an old friend of Soma's who might know how someone could be supplied discreetly. Soma also remembered the restaurant as a comfortable home away from home, with good prices, good food, and what she habitually described to unsuspecting companions as the finest turtle soup in the area.

As the party made their way through the Protector's Enclave, a passerby stopped to stare at Boney, the party's skeletal horse, still wearing Lady Alagondar's tackle. The man was convinced he had seen such a horse before, puzzling over whether it had been outside the walls, on a field, or perhaps a relative of Boney's. The party debated whether skeletal horses had identifiable coloring, children, or siblings. Eventually, after considerable questioning and an Insight check, the man remembered the truth: he had simply seen Boney earlier that same day outside the service where Valphine had delivered her sermon. Vukradin dismissed him with, “Oh, God, you're wasting our time,” then asked the party, “Why don't we talk to strangers? Stop talking to strangers, please.” Soma answered, “Stranger's just a friend you haven't met.”

Their destination was The Board Laid Bare, a modest restaurant in the Tower District that Soma had long considered a home away from home. At the door stood Bellows, a warforged host of mechanical precision and absolute humorlessness. He presented the available seating with clinical exactness: two window tables with good views and a corner table “preferred by guests who do not wish to be observed,” which he recommended. Soma chose a window table because she had guests, while the party immediately began discussing pushing tables together to accommodate Brewbarry.

Bellows recognized Soma and informed her that she had not visited in exactly seventy-three days. He escorted the party to their table and presented a handwritten menu of fresh fish, vegetables, potatoes, and two sauces. There was no alcohol, but the prices were startlingly low compared with the rest of Neverwinter, where disrupted trade routes had driven up the cost of nearly everything. Soma confidently attributed this to locally sourced, farm-to-table food and an independent supply chain within the walls.

Before the party could settle in, Lim — the tortle chef and proprietor — emerged from the kitchen carrying a tray of food she had already decided they wanted. She set it down and joined them. Lim warmly caught up with Soma, who explained that Brewbarry and Vukradin had been taking her “all over creation.” Lim recognized Vukradin by reputation but bluntly told him, “I don't like your music,” explaining that it was not useful to her even though everyone else seemed to love it. She also revealed that the Common Chord, where he used to perform, had become the hottest ticket in town precisely because he once played there.

Lim mentioned that the Tower District had grown quieter in recent weeks, though not safer. Three weeks earlier, people dressed as Many-Starred Cloaks associates had entered the Cloak Tower by the basement stairs, remained there throughout the day, and emerged carrying nothing. Something in the neighborhood had changed.

The party's attention returned to the restaurant's remarkably low prices. Lim claimed only that she had good suppliers and that business remained strong, but she became annoyed and evasive when questioned. She redirected the conversation toward Soma's recent life: the former guide had become a druid of Mielikki carrying a staff that sprouted leaves and was now traveling beside Vukradin and what Lim initially called “a cosplaying Brewbarry.” Soma corrected her: “He's not cosplaying, either. He's… the real deal.”

Vukradin coaxed Lim into offering a kitchen tour by praising her cooking and asking to see how she created it. Soma joined her old friend to exchange recipes and catch up, while Valphine noticed something important about Bellows: the warforged had the look of someone who could not tell a lie. Valphine and Brewbarry therefore stayed behind to question him.

Bellows answered with characteristic precision. Lim refused to advertise because she believed exclusively in word of mouth. The restaurant remained affordable because, in Bellows's words, “We have very good deliveries.” Those deliveries arrived nightly between the third and fourth hour after midnight at the rear door, which opened onto a service lane connecting with the aqueduct. Two men usually carried the crates, sometimes three. They were not always different people; after reviewing the matter aloud, Bellows identified a rotating group of six or seven couriers over the previous thirty days.

The crates themselves were wooden, rope-bound, and wet on the underside. Nine had arrived each night since midsummer, but the restaurant used only eight. Bellows had raised the discrepancy twice. The ninth crate never came inside; it was set down in the lane and gone by morning. It was not billed to the restaurant, and Bellows had never asked what became of it.

Meanwhile, Soma and Lim resumed their old cooking routine. When Soma dipped a finger into the soup, Lim asked whether she still told people the restaurant sold turtle soup. Soma admitted she had told her companions exactly that. Lim laughed and said, “I can't believe they believed you.” The restaurant had once sold turtle soup, but only for a different clientele, and Lim found the continuing stream of customers arriving in search of it hilarious.

Brewbarry then followed Soma into the cramped kitchen, still carrying his halberd, the sword of dragon slaying, and wearing his bathrobe. Under the pretense of being a prospective businessman looking for efficient suppliers, he confronted Lim directly. She initially called her supplier a trade secret, but Brewbarry revealed that Bellows had told him about the ninth crate. Lim insisted, “I don't order nine crates, I order eight,” and claimed she did not know why an extra crate was left outside.

Under continued pressure, Lim admitted that her goods were moved through the sewer system beneath the Blue Lake District. The suppliers had approached her at the market and offered affordable, fair-trade, organic goods outside the official system, allowing her to resist the economic policies and rising costs that were crushing local businesses. She claimed not to know the organization's name or headquarters; she had met a man through another man, paid regularly, and received her food. The sewers were only a transit route, she stressed, not the source of the food.

Lim asked Soma not to scare the suppliers away or ambush them because she had a business to run and could provide good food to her community at prices they could afford. Soma warned that criminals who offered favorable terms often returned with future demands and told Lim she could simply have raised her prices. The reunion soured. Lim said, “You've changed. You're different. I remember a different Soma.” Soma answered that the Lim she remembered would not have become so deeply involved with criminals.

Building on Valphine’s identification of the suppliers as smugglers, the party pieced together the larger picture. Seven tendays ago, the displacement manifold had gone dark and regional distribution had collapsed. Lionshield stopped receiving supplies, House Margaster appeared to control the port, and prices across Neverwinter soared. Yet The Board Laid Bare's prices never moved. Lim's supplier had never depended on the manifold at all. A third trade route existed — one that outlived the manifold, paid no harbor authority, and moved goods at the third hour after midnight through a service lane, aqueduct steps, and the sewers.

The party connected that discovery with Aurelan's advice. Someone was being fed outside the ordinary financial and commercial systems, and the unexplained ninth crate could be sustaining Bimble. They planned to watch the next delivery without frightening Lim's suppliers. Soma considered using an inconspicuous animal form or a conjured familiar, eventually favoring a rat that could remain out of sight while she perceived through it. The party also remembered that sewers attracted rats and possibly were-rats. Their prior business relationship with Zeleen, a were-rat who might know others from Neverwinter, offered another possible route into the underground network.

With hours remaining before the third hour after midnight, the party headed toward the Common Chord. Along the way, Brewbarry shared a newly written marketing song about Lim's turtle soup. Its chorus praised “turtle soup slow simmered just right” and “a bowl of warmth from good ol' Lim's own hand.” The band thanked him for the effort but judged it incompatible with Lathander's Death's artistic direction. Valphine diplomatically classified it as “a marketing jingle.”

The walk through the city was unsettling in small ways. A woman in the Blue Lake District was seen locking a door she had already locked. A wizard's apprentice tried to sell the party authentic Many-Starred Cloak thread, inspiring a lengthy Brewbarry argument that the softness of fabric must logically come from its thread. Citizens in the Protector's Enclave argued loudly — and correctly — that rents had doubled. Rumors circulated that Lord Neverember had taken a drow advisor, prompting immediate concern from Valphine.

Then the party spotted a posted bill advertising a performance by Lathander's Death in two nights at the Common Chord. No member of the band had agreed to it. Vukradin was delighted rather than alarmed: “Definitely be there in two days, that is awesome. They summoned me.”

The Common Chord had transformed entirely since Vukradin's early days. It had once been a modest room that held forty people comfortably, asked few questions, paid actual coin, served cheap booze, and hosted Vukradin for three consecutive nights before audiences averaging approximately eleven people. Petra, the half-elf proprietor, had paid him, though he returned the money of everyone who did not rate his performance ten out of ten. Brewbarry's payment he kept.

Now the venue had a six-week reservation list and an atmosphere of fashionable excitement. Valphine's white dragon plate armor and Brewbarry's imposing appearance drew attention, while several patrons dressed as Brewbarry-style barbarians began chanting when Vukradin announced, “We're Lathander's Death! We're here to perform!” A successful appeal turned uncertainty into screaming enthusiasm.

Petra emerged and proudly proclaimed, “I discovered this guy.” She admitted that the advertised engagement had originally been intended for a cover band that played Vukradin's old songs. She insisted that she always told audiences it was not the real Vukradin. Vukradin nevertheless accused her of profiting from his image and likeness and demanded five hundred gold pieces for the real band's performance. Petra laughed and said she could not afford that much for a single night.

Brewbarry proposed that the band receive fifty percent of the performance’s gross receipts. Petra accepted, shaking on the deal; no immediate five-hundred-gold payment was made. The dispute then broadened into a settlement over Petra's continued use of Vukradin's history, image, and cover band. With Brewbarry looming behind him, Vukradin named five hundred gold as the value of the past infringement and agreed to perform an exclusive show in two nights, allowing Petra to continue using the cover band and his likeness so long as she disclosed that the performers were not the genuine Lathander's Death.

The band performed a one-song teaser that night. Vukradin's performance was mediocre, and Valphine's contribution was disastrously off-key, but the combination worked. The lack of harmony and mistimed screeches perfectly evoked Vukradin's beloved early, discordant, avant-garde sound. What should have been a failure instead delighted the crowd.

Petra had also preserved the table where Vukradin supposedly created his music. It was now polished, surrounded by fine couches, and marked with a plaque reading that Vukradin had composed there, although it was really the table where Brewbarry used to sit. When Petra asked Vukradin to sign it, he replied, “Well, that'll be extra.” Petra paid twenty-five gold pieces immediately. Staring at the newly commercial Vukradin, she asked, “Brewbarry, what did you do to this guy? This guy used to be the… the nicest guy!”

Riding that energy, Brewbarry proposed using the band's popularity to turn public sentiment against House Margaster. The party briefly considered Lord Neverember as a target, then remembered that he was presently their potential ally against Margaster. With Bardic Inspiration and further magical assistance improving the performance, the band delivered a substantially stronger second song. Its witty lyrics attacked House Margaster's trade deals and the inflationary suffering inflicted on ordinary citizens. The crowd began humming and repeating the tune, carrying its message beyond the room.

The party settled in afterward. The Common Chord now served elaborate food despite having offered none in Vukradin's early days, and its beer was far better than the swill Brewbarry had once considered excellent. Brewbarry maintained that anything capable of making him drunk qualified as good, so Petra relegated him to the cheap beer while offering the superior drink to those who appreciated it.

The evening had been productive in ways that went beyond coin. The party had uncovered a smuggling network beneath the city, identified a likely means by which the missing gnome was being fed, prepared a discreet stakeout, negotiated control over Vukradin's burgeoning celebrity, and turned a room full of strangers into unwitting allies against House Margaster — all before the night's delivery had even arrived.

