A central district of Neverwinter where the party encounters a confused passerby who claims to recognize Boney. The stranger eventually realizes that he saw the skeletal horse outside Valphine's service earlier that same day. Elsewhere in the district, citizens are heard complaining that rents have doubled.

### Blue Lake District

An area of Neverwinter where many businesses appear shut and residents exhibit signs of unease, including a woman locking an already locked door. The party encounters a wizard's apprentice selling supposedly authentic Many-Starred Cloak thread. Service lanes in the district connect local buildings to aqueduct access and the sewer network.

### The Board Laid Bare

A local restaurant in the Tower District of Neverwinter known for its affordable food and tortle cuisine, run by the chef Lim and hosted by the warforged Bellows. Soma considers it a home away from home.

The handwritten menu offers fresh fish, vegetables, potatoes, and two sauces, with no alcohol. Its food is excellent despite prices that have remained at approximately pre-disruption levels. Lim does not advertise, relying exclusively on word of mouth.

The restaurant features a small kitchen and a rear door opening onto a service lane connected to the aqueducts. Nine wet-bottomed, rope-bound wooden crates arrive there nightly between the third and fourth hour after midnight, although only eight enter the restaurant.

Contrary to Soma's long-running claims, it does not currently serve turtle soup. Lim admits that it once did so for a different clientele and finds it hilarious that people continue arriving in search of it.

### Tower District

An area of Neverwinter that has recently become quieter but not necessarily safer. Lim distinguishes sharply between those conditions. Three weeks prior, people dressed as Many-Starred Cloaks associates were seen entering the Cloak Tower by the basement stairs and remaining there for the day.

### Cloak Tower

A notable structure in the Tower District where suspicious individuals dressed as Many-Starred Cloaks associates were recently seen entering the basement. They spent an entire day inside and emerged carrying nothing visible.

### Blue Lake District Sewers

An underground transit network used by smugglers to move goods into Neverwinter without paying harbor authority fees or using the port distribution channels the party associates with House Margaster. Lim emphasizes that the food is only transported through the sewers, not sourced from them.

The route appears to connect the sewers, aqueduct steps, a rear service lane, and The Board Laid Bare. The party hopes the route may lead to whoever is feeding Bimble and considers contacting rats or were-rats familiar with the underground network.

### The Common Chord

A tavern and music venue in Neverwinter that has transformed from a struggling dive bar into a trendy, high-end establishment with a six-week reservation list.

The room once held forty patrons comfortably but attracted an average audience of only eleven during Vukradin's original three-night engagement. At that time, it offered no food and served cheap liquor that Brewbarry considered good.

The renovated venue now serves elaborate meals and quality beer. It profits from Vukradin's reputation through cover performances, themed patrons, and a commemorative plaque at the table where he supposedly composed his music — actually the table where Brewbarry used to sit.

## NPCs

### Aurelan Vance

A representative of the Counting House, Brewbarry's banker, and an informant who was originally recruited through an intimidation check. Brewbarry views their relationship as an exchange of favors between friends.

Aurelan is unfamiliar with goliath forms of address. After Brewbarry invites him to use “Brew,” he offers his own familiar name, “Ori.” Inspired by Valphine's sermon, he reviewed Bimble's financial records and rushed to report his discovery.

Aurelan thinks like a banker. The abrupt cessation of regular maintenance payments without severance, reconciliation, or forwarding instructions convinces him that Bimble's contract was abandoned rather than properly ended. His central advice is to identify whoever is feeding the missing gnome.

### Bimble

A gnome who was on a commission maintenance roll for eleven years until payments abruptly stopped seven tendays ago with no severance or forwarding instructions. He has not banked, borrowed, drawn money, or left the city.

The party suspects someone is keeping him locally or that he is surviving outside formal systems, possibly by receiving or stealing the mysterious ninth crate left behind The Board Laid Bare every night. Despite repeated corrections, several party members call him “Bimbo.”

### Boney

the party's skeletal horse, currently wearing Lady Alagondar's tackle. A passerby in the Protector's Enclave becomes convinced that he recognizes Boney, prompting speculation about skeletal-horse markings, posture, offspring, and siblings.

The stranger ultimately remembers seeing Boney outside Valphine's service earlier that same day, making the apparent recognition much less mysterious.

### Bellows

A precise and humorless warforged host at The Board Laid Bare. Bellows remembers exactly how long patrons have been absent, informing Soma that she had not visited for seventy-three days.

Valphine judges Bellows to have the manner of someone incapable of lying. Bellows answers questions literally, tracks operational details with mathematical accuracy, and has no apparent curiosity about matters outside his assigned responsibilities.

He knows the delivery schedule, route, courier rotation, condition of the crates, and exact discrepancy between the nine crates delivered and the eight used. Although he raised the discrepancy twice, he never investigated the ninth crate because it was not brought inside or billed to the restaurant.

### Lim

A tortle chef and proprietor of The Board Laid Bare, and an old friend of Soma's. She cooks excellent raw and steamed tortle cuisine, chooses meals for familiar patrons without waiting for an order, and prefers word-of-mouth business to advertising.

Lim once cooked alongside Soma and is surprised by her transformation from a local guide into a druid of Mielikki traveling with famous adventurers. She dislikes Vukradin's music personally but recognizes his immense popularity.

To preserve her community restaurant and keep food affordable, Lim entered a secret agreement with smugglers who approached her at the market. She orders eight crates of food each night and denies knowing why a ninth appears. She regards her suppliers as necessary partners rather than bad people and fears that party interference will drive them away.

Soma's criticism wounds her deeply, leading Lim to say that her old friend has changed.

### Zeleen

A were-rat with whom the party has a prior business relationship. Because were-rats tend to know one another, Zeleen may provide a lead into the sewer networks beneath Neverwinter or identify were-rats connected to the city and the Mountain's Toe Gold Mine.

### Petra

A half-elf proprietor of the Common Chord who gave Vukradin his start when no one else would book him. She once hosted him for three nights before tiny audiences, paid him despite mixed reactions, and told him she enjoyed his music without offering another booking.

Petra has since capitalized on Vukradin's growing fame by transforming the Common Chord into a fashionable venue with a six-week reservation list. She employs a cover band to play his older material, preserves his former table as a shrine, and advertises performances associated with Lathander's Death while claiming to disclose that the performers are not the genuine band.

She refuses to pay five hundred gold pieces directly for a one-night performance but settles the dispute through an exclusive appearance in two nights, a payment to the band of fifty percent of the show’s gross receipts, and a perpetual licence to continue using Vukradin's image and cover material with disclosure. She also pays twenty-five gold pieces immediately for Vukradin's signature on the commemorative plaque.

## Items

### Lady Alagondar's Tackle

The riding gear currently worn by Boney, the party's skeletal horse. A passerby in the Protector's Enclave considers the tackle and Boney's posture while trying to understand why the horse looks familiar. He ultimately identifies Boney as the horse he saw outside Valphine's service earlier that day.

### The Ninth Crate

A mysterious wooden, rope-bound crate delivered nightly to the rear of The Board Laid Bare along with eight others. Like the other crates, it is wet on the underside, consistent with transit through the aqueduct or sewer route.

The restaurant uses only eight crates. The ninth is set down outside, never brought into the kitchen, not billed to Lim, and gone by morning. Bellows has raised the discrepancy twice but has not investigated further.

The party suspects the crate is deliberately being used to feed someone outside the ordinary commercial system, possibly Bimble.

### Many-Starred Cloak Thread

Decorative thread sold by a wizard's apprentice in the Blue Lake District and claimed to be authentic material associated with the Many-Starred Cloaks.

Brewbarry asks whether it is soft and argues that thread must determine fabric softness because fabric is made from thread. Valphine clarifies that it is decorative.

### Vukradin's Plaque

A commemorative plaque placed on a polished table at the Common Chord, marking it as the spot where Vukradin created his music. The table was actually where Brewbarry used to sit.

When Petra asks Vukradin to sign the plaque, he demands payment. Petra pays twenty-five gold pieces immediately, seeing additional commercial value in the authenticated memorabilia.

